@h1 Troubleshooting h1@

A red error is not a scolding — it's a signpost. Wit's errors point at an exact
line and column and name what went wrong, and almost all of them have a
one-line fix. This page walks the errors newcomers actually hit, roughly in the
order they tend to meet them, each as @strong symptom → cause → fix strong@. Once
you've seen them here, they stop being scary.

@h2 How Wit reports an error h2@

Every error prints in the same shape — file, line, column, code, message:

@@@pre
report.wit:3:1: E_MISMATCHED_CLOSE: expected aside@ but got note@
pre@@@

The @code E_ code@ code is stable and searchable; the message tells you what the
parser expected. Two commands surface these. @code wit check report.wit code@
reports errors without building anything — run it whenever a build looks off.
@code wit build code@ reports the same errors and refuses to emit until they're
fixed. Reach for @code check code@ first; it's the fastest way to a green file.

@h2 Unclosed node h2@

@strong Symptom.strong@

@@@pre
report.wit:1:1: E_UNCLOSED_NODE: unclosed @aside
pre@@@

@strong Cause.strong@ You opened @@@code @aside code@@@ and never wrote the matching
@@@code aside@ code@@@. The location points at the @em opener em@, so you know
exactly which node lost its close.

@strong Fix.strong@ Add the closer. Remember the core mechanic is a @strong pair strong@,
not indentation — a node stays open until its @@@code name@ code@@@ appears,
however you indent it.

@h2 Mismatched close h2@

@strong Symptom.strong@

@@@pre
report.wit:3:1: E_MISMATCHED_CLOSE: expected aside@ but got note@
pre@@@

@strong Cause.strong@ The close name doesn't match the open — here you opened
@@@code @aside code@@@ but closed with @@@code note@ code@@@. The location points at
the @em closer em@.

@strong Fix.strong@ Make the names match. If you're nesting several nodes, remember
closes are last-in-first-out: close the innermost node first.

@h2 Unresolved reference h2@

@strong Symptom.strong@

@@@pre
report.wit:1:5: E_UNRESOLVED_REFERENCE: Unresolved reference @glossary
pre@@@

@strong Cause.strong@ You used @@@code @glossary code@@@ but nothing defines it — no
@@@code #glossary code@@@ definition, and it isn't a built-in node. Usually a typo,
or a missing reference to another file.

@strong Fix.strong@ Define it, fix the spelling, or add the reference to the file it
lives in.

@aside(class note) 💡 An important nuance. An unknown @em name em@ is a hard
error, as above. But a missing @em field on a name that @strong does strong@
resolve is @strong lenient strong@: @@@code @keeper.tenure code@@@, when
@@@code #keeper code@@@ exists but has no @code tenure code@ field, renders as
@@@code <span class="wit-unresolved">@keeper.tenure</span> code@@@ and the build
@strong succeeds strong@. So "my build passed but the text looks wrong" is almost
always a bad field path, not a bad name. Search your output for
@code wit-unresolved code@ to find them. aside@

@h2 A heading or block comes out wrongly nested h2@

@strong Symptom.strong@ No error at all — but a heading or block inside a node
renders swallowed into a stray paragraph, like
@@@code <aside><p><h3>Heading</h3> … </p></aside> code@@@.

@strong Cause.strong@ A @code ~ code@ comment as the @em first line em@ of a
node's body, with no blank line after it, collapses the following block into one
paragraph. It's a known parser quirk, and it fails silently — which is what makes
it confusing.

@strong Fix.strong@ Leave a blank line after any comment inside a node:

@@@pre
@aside
  ~ a note to myself

  @h3 Heading h3@
  A paragraph.
aside@
pre@@@

@h2 Malformed record from a comma in a value h2@

@strong Symptom.strong@

@@@pre
report.wit:1:29: E_MALFORMED_RECORD: record field missing key
pre@@@

@strong Cause.strong@ Record fields in @@@code { … } code@@@ are comma-separated, and
there is @strong no way to quote a comma inside a value strong@. So
@@@code { title - Attention, Perception } code@@@ reads @code Perception code@ as a
second, keyless field — and the parser complains the field has no key.

@strong Fix.strong@ Escape the comma as @@@code \, code@@@ —
@@@code { title - Attention\, Perception } code@@@ builds and renders the comma. Or
restructure to avoid the comma, or use a form-fill body (below), where commas in
values are safe.

@h2 Form-fill line isn't key-and-value h2@

@strong Symptom.strong@

@@@pre
report.wit:1:1: E_MALFORMED_FORM_FIELD: form-fill body line is not <key>:<value>: this line has no colon
pre@@@

@strong Cause.strong@ Inside a form-fill body, every line must be
@code key: value code@. A line of plain prose — or a stray note — isn't a field,
and the parser says so.

@strong Fix.strong@ Make each line @code key: value code@, or switch to a different
invocation form (parameters or a record argument) if the body isn't really a set
of fields.

@h2 Verbatim fences that don't match h2@

@strong Symptom.strong@

@@@pre
report.wit:1:1: E_UNCLOSED_RAW_NODE: unclosed @@pre (expected pre@@)
pre@@@

@strong Cause.strong@ A verbatim node opened with a fence of one width and never
closed with the same width. A two-@code @ code@ node @@@code @@pre code@@@ closes
with @@@code pre@@ code@@@; a three-@code @ code@ frozen node
@@@code @@@pre code@@@ closes with @@@code pre@@@ code@@@.

@strong Fix.strong@ Match the fence width. Use the two-@code @ code@ form when you
want @@@code {{path}} code@@@ interpolation inside the body, and the three-@code @ code@
form when you want the body completely frozen, interpolation and all.

@h2 A data reference cuts off at a space h2@

@strong Symptom.strong@ @@@code @keeper.years at post code@@@ renders
@@@code <span class="wit-unresolved">@keeper.years</span> at post code@@@ — only the
first word resolved.

@strong Cause.strong@ A space ends a field name, so @@@code @keeper.years at post code@@@
looks up the field @code years code@ and leaves @code at post code@ as prose. If the
field you meant is @code years at post code@, Wit never sees the whole name.

@strong Fix.strong@ Use single-word or @code snake_case code@ keys:
@@@code @keeper.years_at_post code@@@ resolves cleanly. Name your record fields
without spaces and this never bites.

@h2 A conditional that silently misfires h2@

@strong Symptom.strong@ @@@code (if @tags contains urgent) code@@@ prints the literal
words @code contains urgent code@ @em and em@ shows the body regardless of the
data.

@strong Cause.strong@ Conditionals understand only @code is code@ and
@code equals code@ (synonyms) and a bare truthy check. There is no
@code contains code@, no @code < code@ or @code > code@, no @code and code@ /
@code or code@ / @code not code@. Anything else isn't an operator, so the
comparison never happens.

@strong Fix.strong@ Restructure to an @code is code@ check or a truthy flag — for
example, precompute a boolean field on your data and test that:
@@@code (if @post.is_urgent) code@@@.

@h2 The full catalogue h2@

This page covers the errors you'll actually meet early. The complete list of
codes — including @code E_UNCLOSED_COMMENT code@, @code E_UNCLOSED_PAREN code@,
@code E_UNCLOSED_DEFINITION code@, @code E_EMPTY_PIPE code@,
@code E_MISSING_REFERENCE_FILE code@, @code E_CIRCULAR_REFERENCE code@,
@code E_PARTIAL_SHAPE_MISMATCH code@, @code E_BARE_FIELD code@, and
@code E_TYPE_MISMATCH code@ — lives in the reference. As a rule, the location is
the opener for an unclosed thing, the closer for a mismatch, the reference site
for an unresolved name, and the field name for a missing field.

@h2 Key points h2@

@ul
@li Most errors point at an exact @code line:col code@ — start there. li@
@li The mechanic is @strong pairs strong@, not indentation; an unclosed or mismatched close is the most common early error. li@
@li Run @code wit check code@ before @code wit build code@ to see errors fast. li@
@li A build that @em passes em@ but reads wrong is usually a lenient unresolved field path — search the output for @code wit-unresolved code@. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

For the mechanic behind most of these, read
@a |href /docs/start/nodes/| Nodes and core vocabulary a@ and
@a |href /docs/start/mental-model/| the mental model a@. The
@a |href /docs/reference/core-vocabulary/| reference a@ lists every built-in node.
Migrating from another tool? @a |href /docs/start/migration-markdown/| Markdown a@
and @a |href /docs/start/migration-latex/| LaTeX a@ each flag the mistakes that
tool's muscle memory causes.
